<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anne of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1461">1461</a>–<a href="/years/1522">1522</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Anne of Beaujeu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was the eldest daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a>. With her husband Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaujeu">Beaujeu</a>, later Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, she governed France during the minority of her brother Charles VIII from 1483, defeating the princes' revolt known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mad-war">Mad War</a> and arranging his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-of-brittany">Anne of Brittany</a>. She was one of the most powerful women of her age and wrote a book of lessons for her daughter.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anne-of-france.jpg" alt="Anne of France" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Hey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anne_Beaujeu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
